From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Renê de Souza Pinto <rene@renesp.com.br>
Date: Wed, 24 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] vfio/igd: hold display power wells on for an assigned iGPU

Companion to the DC-state clamp (patch 18).  With DC5/DC6/DC9 denied the
display engine still froze the host after some time under a guest input
workload (typing / mouse on the login screen), which points at the other
display power-down path the DC clamp does not cover: the power wells.

PWR_WELL_CTL_DRIVER (BAR0 0x45404) gates blocks of the display engine.
Each well has a REQUEST bit (odd, writable) the driver sets to ask the well
on or off, and a STATE bit (even, read-only) the hardware reports back.  On
an assigned iGPU the guest drives these directly.  When it powers a well
down and an asynchronous display access lands while that well is off or
mid-transition - a vblank/interrupt handler reading a register in the well,
or a hardware-cursor update driven by user input - the access never
completes and the host stops dead, with no MCE, no panic, no watchdog and
nothing in any log.  This is the same silent-hang signature as the DC-state
path; the hangs in the qemu traces died reading GEN8_MASTER_IRQ inside an
ordinary 60 Hz vblank handler, and input is simply the most efficient
trigger because every keystroke and mouse move forces a wake-then-access.

Clamp the power-DOWN.  On each 32-bit write, OR in the wells that are
currently requested, so any REQUEST bit that is already set stays set while
the guest can still turn additional wells on.  Wells therefore only ever
accumulate on and never drop, so there is no powered-down window for an
async access to race.  Reads pass through untouched.

Like the DC-state clamp this trades display idle power for the hang.  Unlike
DC states, power wells are functional state the driver expects to toggle, so
a driver may observe STATE not matching the off it requested; drivers
tolerate this (i915 warns and continues).  If a modeset misbehaves, narrow
IGD_PWR_WELL_REQ_MASK to the specific wells that race - the qemu trace's
last write to 0x45404 before a hang names them - rather than all of them,
and/or extend the same quirk to PWR_WELL_CTL_DDI (0x45454) / _AUX (0x45444)
if the racing access is in one of those.

0x45404 shares the BAR0 page (0x45000) that the DBUF_CTL sanitize quirk and
the DC-state clamp already trap, so this adds no new vmexits.

Signed-off-by: Renê de Souza Pinto <rene@renesp.com.br>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 hw/vfio/igd.c | 87 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 87 insertions(+)

diff --git a/tools/qemu-xen/hw/vfio/igd.c b/tools/qemu-xen/hw/vfio/igd.c
index b1d40e3f27..c2e5107a44 100644
--- a/tools/qemu-xen/hw/vfio/igd.c
+++ b/tools/qemu-xen/hw/vfio/igd.c
@@ -6,6 +6,71 @@
     .endianness = DEVICE_LITTLE_ENDIAN,
 };
 
+/*
+ * IGD BAR0 PWR_WELL_CTL clamp quirk.
+ *
+ * The display power wells gate blocks of the display engine.  Each well has a
+ * REQUEST bit (odd, writable) the driver sets to ask the well on/off and a
+ * STATE bit (even, read-only) the hardware reports.  On an assigned iGPU the
+ * guest powers wells down itself; if an asynchronous display access - a
+ * vblank/IRQ handler, or an input-driven cursor update - lands while a well is
+ * off or mid-transition, that access never completes and the host dies
+ * silently, the same way a DC-state entry does.
+ *
+ * Hold the wells on: OR the currently-requested wells back into every guest
+ * write, so a REQUEST bit once set never clears while the guest can still turn
+ * more wells on.  Wells only accumulate on, so there is no powered-down window
+ * to race.  Reads pass through.  Cost is display idle power, not function.
+ */
+#define IGD_PWR_WELL_CTL_DRIVER   0x45404
+#define IGD_PWR_WELL_REQ_MASK     0xAAAAAAAAu   /* odd bits: per-well REQUEST */
+
+typedef struct IGDPwrWellQuirk {
+    VFIOPCIDevice *vdev;
+    uint32_t bar_offset;
+    uint8_t bar;
+} IGDPwrWellQuirk;
+
+static uint64_t igd_pwr_well_read(void *opaque, hwaddr addr, unsigned size)
+{
+    IGDPwrWellQuirk *q = opaque;
+    VFIOPCIDevice *vdev = q->vdev;
+
+    return vfio_region_read(&vdev->bars[q->bar].region,
+                            addr + q->bar_offset, size);
+}
+
+static void igd_pwr_well_write(void *opaque, hwaddr addr,
+                               uint64_t data, unsigned size)
+{
+    IGDPwrWellQuirk *q = opaque;
+    VFIOPCIDevice *vdev = q->vdev;
+    uint64_t clamped = data;
+
+    if (size == 4) {
+        uint32_t cur = vfio_region_read(&vdev->bars[q->bar].region,
+                                        addr + q->bar_offset, 4);
+
+        clamped = data | (cur & IGD_PWR_WELL_REQ_MASK);
+        if (clamped != data) {
+            error_report_once("IGD quirk: holding display power wells on "
+                              "(guest wrote 0x%" PRIx64 ", forcing 0x%" PRIx64
+                              "); wells stay powered while the iGPU is assigned",
+                              data, clamped);
+        }
+    }
+
+    vfio_region_write(&vdev->bars[q->bar].region,
+                      addr + q->bar_offset, clamped, size);
+}
+
+static const MemoryRegionOps igd_pwr_well_ops = {
+    .read = igd_pwr_well_read,
+    .write = igd_pwr_well_write,
+    .endianness = DEVICE_LITTLE_ENDIAN,
+};
+
+
 void vfio_probe_igd_bar0_quirk(VFIOPCIDevice *vdev, int nr)
 {
     int gen = igd_gen(vdev);
@@ -25,6 +90,28 @@
                                             &dcquirk->mem[0], 1);
         QLIST_INSERT_HEAD(&vdev->bars[nr].quirks, dcquirk, next);
     }
+
+    /*
+     * PWR_WELL_CTL clamp (gen9+): keep the guest from powering the display
+     * power wells down, closing the window where an async display access races
+     * a well that is off or mid-transition and wedges the host.  See
+     * igd_pwr_well_write().
+     */
+    if (gen >= 9) {
+        VFIOQuirk *pwquirk = vfio_quirk_alloc(1);
+        IGDPwrWellQuirk *pq = pwquirk->data = g_malloc0(sizeof(*pq));
+
+        pq->vdev = vdev;
+        pq->bar = nr;
+        pq->bar_offset = IGD_PWR_WELL_CTL_DRIVER;
+        memory_region_init_io(&pwquirk->mem[0], OBJECT(vdev),
+                              &igd_pwr_well_ops, pq,
+                              "vfio-igd-pwr-well-quirk", 4);
+        memory_region_add_subregion_overlap(vdev->bars[nr].region.mem,
+                                            IGD_PWR_WELL_CTL_DRIVER,
+                                            &pwquirk->mem[0], 1);
+        QLIST_INSERT_HEAD(&vdev->bars[nr].quirks, pwquirk, next);
+    }
 }
 
 void vfio_probe_igd_bar4_quirk(VFIOPCIDevice *vdev, int nr)
